{
 "ep": 20,
 "total": 140,
 "title": "GRANT, REVOKE and Default Privileges",
 "phase": "Installation & Administration",
 "lines": [
  {
   "say": "Why can't my user read tomorrow's tables?",
   "cap": "Why can't my user read *tomorrow's tables?*"
  },
  {
   "say": "This is episode twenty of the PostgreSQL DBA series. Today: GRANT, REVOKE, and default privileges.",
   "cap": "Episode 20: *GRANT, REVOKE,* and *default privileges.*",
   "cue": "default"
  },
  {
   "say": "Every new table has one owner. At first, only the owner or a superuser can use it.",
   "cap": "New table: only the *owner* can use it.",
   "cue": "owner"
  },
  {
   "say": "GRANT gives a role privileges such as SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE. REVOKE takes them away.",
   "cap": "*GRANT* gives privileges. *REVOKE* removes them.",
   "cue": "REVOKE"
  },
  {
   "say": "PUBLIC means every role, even future ones. By default, PUBLIC gets nothing on tables, schemas or sequences.",
   "cap": "*PUBLIC* gets *nothing* on tables by default.",
   "cue": "nothing"
  },
  {
   "say": "But PUBLIC does get connect and temporary on databases, and execute on functions and procedures.",
   "cap": "Except *CONNECT, TEMPORARY* and *EXECUTE.*",
   "cue": "execute"
  },
  {
   "say": "Let's test. As app_owner, we grant usage on schema app, and SELECT on all tables in schema app, to report_user.",
   "cap": "Grant *USAGE* and *SELECT ON ALL TABLES.*",
   "cue": "SELECT"
  },
  {
   "say": "psql's backslash dp shows it. Output trimmed: report_user equals r, slash app_owner.",
   "cap": "*\\dp* shows report_user=*r*/app_owner",
   "cue": "report_user"
  },
  {
   "say": "r is SELECT, and after the slash comes the grantor. The owner holds all seven table privileges.",
   "cap": "Letters = *privileges.* After the slash: *grantor.*",
   "cue": "seven"
  },
  {
   "say": "Then app_owner creates a new table, invoices. report_user gets permission denied for table invoices.",
   "cap": "New table invoices: *permission denied.*",
   "cue": "permission"
  },
  {
   "say": "That's the trap. GRANT on all tables changes only the tables that exist when you run it. Nothing created later.",
   "cap": "*ALL TABLES* = tables that exist *right now.*",
   "cue": "later"
  },
  {
   "say": "For future tables, use alter default privileges. In schema app, grant SELECT on tables to report_user.",
   "cap": "*ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES* for future tables.",
   "cue": "grant"
  },
  {
   "say": "Backslash ddp lists the new default for report_user. Output trimmed.",
   "cap": "*\\ddp* lists default privileges.",
   "cue": "default"
  },
  {
   "say": "app_owner creates refunds, and report_user can read it at once.",
   "cap": "New table refunds: *readable.*",
   "cue": "read"
  },
  {
   "say": "But invoices is still denied: defaults never change existing objects. Grant on it once, by hand.",
   "cap": "Defaults never fix *existing* tables.",
   "cue": "hand"
  },
  {
   "say": "Now REVOKE. A role's access is the sum of its own grants, its memberships, and PUBLIC.",
   "cap": "Access = *own grants + memberships + PUBLIC.*",
   "cue": "PUBLIC"
  },
  {
   "say": "We granted SELECT on orders to PUBLIC, then revoked it from report_user. report_user could still read orders.",
   "cap": "Revoked from user, *still readable* via PUBLIC.",
   "cue": "still"
  },
  {
   "say": "Only after we revoked it from PUBLIC too did report_user get permission denied.",
   "cap": "Revoke from *PUBLIC* too: *denied.*",
   "cue": "denied"
  },
  {
   "say": "Grant option chains: analyst got SELECT with grant option, and granted it to report_user.",
   "cap": "*WITH GRANT OPTION* lets analyst re-grant.",
   "cue": "report_user"
  },
  {
   "say": "A plain revoke from analyst fails: dependent privileges exist. With CASCADE, the dependent grant goes too.",
   "cap": "*dependent privileges exist:* use *CASCADE.*",
   "cue": "CASCADE"
  },
  {
   "say": "And you can only revoke privileges that you granted directly.",
   "cap": "You revoke only *what you granted.*",
   "cue": "directly"
  },
  {
   "say": "A role that still holds grants cannot be dropped. drop role report_user failed: some objects depend on it.",
   "cap": "*DROP ROLE* fails while grants remain.",
   "cue": "failed"
  },
  {
   "say": "Revoke them first. drop owned by revokes them too, but it also drops every object the role owns.",
   "cap": "*DROP OWNED* also *drops owned objects.*",
   "cue": "drops"
  },
  {
   "say": "Recap: GRANT and REVOKE change existing objects. Default privileges shape future ones. Access adds up, PUBLIC included.",
   "cap": "Recap: *existing* vs *future* objects.",
   "cue": "Default"
  },
  {
   "say": "Next up: pg_hba.conf, who may connect. Follow so you don't miss it, and send this to a friend who's learning PostgreSQL.",
   "cap": "Next up: *pg_hba.conf.* *Follow* and share."
  }
 ],
